<h2>Two doctrines in tension</h2>
<p>The first doctrine is restraint: where an effective alternative remedy exists, the writ court ordinarily does not interfere — a rule the Supreme Court has pressed with particular force in commercial and recovery contexts, notably in <em>United Bank of India v. Satyawati Tondon</em> (2010). The second is supervision: the State remains the State even when it signs a contract, and its actions must satisfy Article 14 at every stage — a line running from <em>Gujarat State Financial Corporation v. Lotus Hotels</em> (1983) through <em>ABL International v. ECGC</em> (2004) to the modern tender jurisprudence. An arbitration clause sits exactly at the junction of the two.</p>

<h2>The working map</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Nature of the grievance</th><th>Likely forum</th></tr>
<tr><td>Arbitrary or mala fide cancellation of a concluded tender or contract by the State</td><td>Writ court — Article 14 scrutiny despite the clause</td></tr>
<tr><td>Violation of natural justice in blacklisting or debarment</td><td>Writ court — blacklisting is civil death, tested on fairness</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disputed quality, quantity, delay or accounts under the contract</td><td>Arbitration — factual disputes need evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Money claim where liability is admitted or facts undisputed, refusal arbitrary</td><td>Writ possible (ABL International), though discretion is cautious</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interim protection of the subject matter pending arbitration</td><td>Section 9 petition, not Article 226</td></tr>
<tr><td>Challenge to the award or the tribunal's jurisdiction</td><td>Sections 16, 34 and 37 — the Act's own channels, writs barred save rare cases</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The recognised exceptions to the alternative-remedy rule</h2>
<p>The exceptions crystallised in <em>Whirlpool Corporation v. Registrar of Trade Marks</em> (1998) govern here as elsewhere: a writ petition may be entertained despite an alternative remedy where enforcement of fundamental rights is sought, where natural justice has been violated, where the proceedings are wholly without jurisdiction, or where the vires of an enactment is challenged. To these, the State-contract cases add the Article 14 dimension — arbitrariness in the State's dealings is a public-law wrong that an arbitrator, confined to the contract, is poorly placed to remedy.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>The department's objection</h3><p>"Clause 14 provides arbitration. The petition is not maintainable; the contractor must invoke the clause."</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>The correct frame</h3><p>"The petition is maintainable; the question is whether it should be entertained. If the challenge is to arbitrary State action on undisputed facts, it should. If the dispute is contractual fact, relegate."</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>Choosing the door:</strong> plead the public-law wrong, not the breach — the cancellation without reasons, the hearing never given, the discrimination between identically placed bidders. Keep the factual matrix within documents; the moment the case needs witnesses, the writ court's discretion tilts away. File early: delay feeds the discretion against entertaining. And protect the arbitration remedy in parallel — invoke the clause within limitation, so that relegation, if it comes, costs time but not the claim.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Within arbitration's own field the writ door is nearly shut: challenges to arbitral interim orders or awards travel under Sections 34 and 37 of the 1996 Act, and Article 226 or 227 interference is reserved for exceptional perversity. The choice discussed here arises before arbitration begins — when the State's conduct, not the contract's performance, is the real grievance.</p>
</div>
